# Keep the Composer classmap current

`vendor/` is committed, and production hosts never run Composer. The optimised classmap in `vendor/composer/` is what a release ships. A class added under `src/` without a rebuilt classmap is missing from that release, and `bin/build-release.php` refuses to build.

The project has no third-party packages, only PHP and extension requirements. There is nothing to `composer update`. The step that matters on every commit is the classmap.

Before every commit that adds, renames, moves, or deletes a PHP file under `src/`, or edits `composer.json`:

```text
composer dump-autoload -o
```

- Use `php composer.phar dump-autoload -o` where `composer` is not installed. Set `COMPOSER_ALLOW_SUPERUSER=1` when running as root.
- Stage `vendor/composer/autoload_*.php` in the same commit as the source change.
- Do not stage `vendor/composer/installed.php`. It only records the current commit hash and changes on every run. Restore it with `git checkout -- vendor/composer/installed.php`.
- The `.githooks/pre-commit` hook does all of this. `.cursor/prepare-runtime.sh` turns it on for cloud sessions with `git config core.hooksPath .githooks`. Run the same command once in a local clone.
- Never run `composer update` or `composer require` to add a package without being asked. Releases are zips of this repository, and any new package must be committed under `vendor/`.
